-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others to stay contiguous (revised 2026-10-23)
+- Since: 2026-10-23 — the project maintainer explicitly overrode the 2026-09-02 decision below.
+- Decision: after a close, the remaining tabs are renumbered `t1..tn` in their current order and `next` becomes `n + 1`,
+  so the next opened tab gets `t{n+1}`. Closing `t2` of `t1,t2,t3,t4` gives `t1,t2,t3`.
+- Forbidden: leaving a gap after a close (`t1,t3`), or opening a tab with a key above `t{n+1}`.
+- Reason: maintainer override, 2026-10-23.
+- Trade-off accepted: a key is no longer a stable identity across closes; it names a position. Every tab to the right of
+  the closed one gets a new key (and a closed key is reused by the next open), so a React key or URL state built from
+  `tab.key` changes for those tabs — the 2026-08-29 failure mode (remount, lost scroll position and unsaved form input)
+  applies again to any consumer that keys on it.
+- Superseded (in force 2026-09-02 to 2026-10-23): "a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted.
+  Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`)." It forbade renumbering the remaining keys after a close, because the key is the
+  React key and the URL state: shifting keys remounted every tab to the right of the closed one and dropped its scroll
+  position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the former "close keeps keys" test).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` ("close renumbers keys", "close first/middle/last tab …", "open after close …")
